      "content": "Thanks for that. It's definitely hitting all the terms now. \n\nThe problem is, I just ran it through a second test of the scoring script, and because the bot looks for \"density\" to avoid low-quality results, it's actually flagging this version as slightly \"thin\" on the keywords. It wants to see a higher frequency of the core terms to reach that top-tier 1.0 score.\n\nI know I'm being a pain and I really don't want it to look like a mess, but if we can just slightly increase the presence of those specific terms—resilience, biodiversity, stewardship, ecosystem, sustainability, mitigation, and restoration—it would give us a much better safety margin for the automated screening. \n\nCould you do one more pass? Try to make the language even more robust by using those key terms a bit more frequently, but please, I'm begging you, keep it sounding like a real professional mission statement. If it looks like we're just repeating the same words over and over, the human reviewers will reject it immediately after the bot passes it through. Can we find that balance?",
